+
+  On PostgreSQL, SQL transaction boundaries and the runner's advisory-lock lifetime are
+  separate controls. For `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, the
+  [transactional-lock setting](https://documentation.red-gate.com/flyway/reference/configuration/flyway-namespace/flyway-postgresql-namespace/flyway-postgresql-transactional-lock-setting)
+  documents `flyway.postgresql.transactional.lock=false`, selecting session-level locks.
+  This changes lock scope; it does not disable mutual exclusion or replace the SQL's
+  nontransactional execution requirement. The
+  [Flyway 11.9.1 implementation](https://github.com/flyway/flyway/blob/flyway-11.9.1/flyway-database/flyway-database-postgresql/src/main/java/org/flywaydb/database/postgresql/PostgreSQLAdvisoryLockTemplate.java)
+  acquires and explicitly releases a session advisory lock on this branch.
+
+  Trace the actual migration connection through any pooler, including effective per-database
+  or per-user overrides. [PgBouncer transaction pooling](https://www.pgbouncer.org/features.html)
+  does not support session-level advisory locks. Use a direct or session-pooled migrator path
+  that preserves the backend session for the lock's lifetime, with the same intended
+  database/schema/history and an accounted connection budget. Retain an already suitable
+  path; this is not a reason to change the application's pool globally. Rehearse competing
+  migrators and failure cleanup on that path, then inspect catalog and history before retrying;
+  nontransactional DDL and session locks do not make partial results disappear.
